 <p class="article-subtitle">A research commons for consciousness</p>
-<p class="article-meta">January 2026 • version 0.1</p>
-<p><em>We mapped the genome to understand the body. Now we must map lived experience so inner development can compound instead of disappear: each person’s hard-won insight improving the protocols that help the next person suffer less, act with greater agency, and go further.</em></p>
+<p class="article-meta">January 2026</p>
+<p><em>We mapped the genome to understand the body. Now we must map lived experience so each person’s hard-won insight improves the protocols available to the next. That accumulated knowledge can help people suffer less, act with greater agency, and go further.</em></p>
 <h1>The Measurement Problem</h1>
-<p>A meditation practitioner sits for ten years, accumulating thousands of hours of practice. They report profound shifts in well-being, focus, and emotional regulation. Yet they cannot demonstrate these changes objectively. They cannot share their methodology with precision. They cannot replicate their results reliably. Most critically, they cannot contribute their hard-won insights to collective knowledge in ways that advance the field.</p>
-<p>This isn't a story about meditation specifically. Replace "meditation" with any consciousness intervention—psychedelic therapy, breathwork, neurofeedback, contemplative practice—and the pattern repeats. Individual practitioners achieve genuine transformation. The field accumulates no knowledge. Science makes no progress. The next practitioner starts from zero.</p>
-<p>The problem isn't that consciousness research lacks funding, though it does. The problem isn't that practices lack efficacy, though rigor varies. The fundamental problem is architectural: <strong>consciousness research operates without infrastructure for systematic data collection, protocol distribution, and knowledge accumulation.</strong></p>
-<p>We have built the internet, enabling billions to exchange information instantly. We have built blockchains, enabling strangers to transact without intermediaries. We have built machine learning infrastructure, enabling algorithms to improve through data exposure. Yet the most important research frontier—understanding and optimizing human consciousness—operates through anecdotes, guru-disciple relationships, and isolated academic studies that rarely replicate.</p>
+<p>A meditation practitioner sits for ten years, accumulating thousands of hours of practice. They report profound shifts in well-being, focus, and emotional regulation. Yet they cannot demonstrate these changes objectively, share their methodology precisely, or reliably replicate their results. Their insights cannot contribute to collective knowledge in ways that advance the field.</p>
+<p>The same pattern repeats across consciousness interventions: psychedelic therapy, breathwork, neurofeedback, and contemplative practice. Individual practitioners achieve genuine transformation, but the field accumulates no knowledge and science makes no progress. The next practitioner starts from zero.</p>
+<p>Consciousness research needs better infrastructure for its existing participants. There are millions of meditation practitioners, thousands of psychedelic therapists, and hundreds of neurofeedback clinics. Their practical knowledge is lost because no infrastructure records it systematically.</p>
+<p>Consciousness research lacks funding, and practices vary in rigor despite their efficacy. Its fundamental problem is architectural: <strong>consciousness research operates without infrastructure for systematic data collection, protocol distribution, and knowledge accumulation.</strong></p>
+<p>The internet lets billions exchange information instantly, while blockchains let strangers transact without intermediaries. Machine learning infrastructure enables algorithms to improve through data exposure. Research into understanding and optimizing human consciousness still relies on anecdotes, guru-disciple relationships, and isolated academic studies that rarely replicate.</p>
 <h1>From Subjective to Systemic</h1>
-<p>Consider how modern medicine evolved from craft to science. Individual physicians accumulated clinical experience, but medicine didn't advance systematically until infrastructure emerged for systematic data collection, controlled trials, peer review, and protocol replication. The infrastructure came first. The knowledge accumulation followed.</p>
-<p>Consciousness research requires analogous infrastructure. Not clinical trial infrastructure—that exists but addresses different questions. Not meditation app infrastructure—that scales distribution but not knowledge. What's needed is infrastructure that transforms subjective experience into objective protocol, individual practice into collective dataset, isolated insight into systematic knowledge.</p>
+<p>Individual physicians accumulated clinical experience long before medicine advanced systematically. That advance required infrastructure for systematic data collection, controlled trials, peer review, and protocol replication. The infrastructure allowed knowledge to accumulate beyond an individual physician’s practice.</p>
+<p>Consciousness research requires analogous infrastructure. Existing clinical trials address different questions, while meditation apps scale distribution without accumulating knowledge. Research needs a way to turn subjective experience into objective protocols, individual practice into collective datasets, and isolated insights into systematic knowledge.</p>
 <p>Sensoria provides this infrastructure through three integrated layers:</p>
-<p><strong>1. Network Layer</strong> — A decentralized coordination network connecting consciousness practitioners, researchers, and protocol designers. Unlike meditation apps (which are consumer products) or academic institutions (which are credentialing bodies), Sensoria operates as a research commons where participants contribute to collective knowledge while retaining ownership of their data.</p>
-<p><strong>2. Data Layer</strong> — A cryptographically secured data DAO that holds all research data in zero-knowledge encrypted form. Practitioners control access to their individual data. Researchers can analyze aggregate patterns without accessing individual records. The DAO ensures data persistence beyond any single institution's lifespan while maintaining privacy guarantees impossible in traditional research settings.</p>
-<p><strong>3. Protocol Layer</strong> — A distribution system for consciousness intervention protocols that enables systematic experimentation, A/B testing, and iterative refinement. Protocols are versioned, attributed, and trackable. Practitioners receive protocols as structured programs with built-in measurement frameworks. Outcomes feed back into aggregate analysis, creating systematic improvement loops.</p>
+<p><strong>1. Network Layer</strong> — A decentralized coordination network connects consciousness practitioners, researchers, and protocol designers. Meditation apps operate as consumer products, and academic institutions as credentialing bodies. Sensoria instead operates as a research commons where participants contribute to collective knowledge while retaining ownership of their data.</p>
+<p><strong>2. Data Layer</strong> — A cryptographically secured data DAO holds all research data in zero-knowledge encrypted form. Practitioners control access to their individual data, and researchers can analyze aggregate patterns without accessing individual records. The DAO preserves data beyond any single institution’s lifespan, with privacy guarantees impossible in traditional research settings.</p>
+<p><strong>3. Protocol Layer</strong> — A distribution system for consciousness intervention protocols enables systematic experimentation, A/B testing, and iterative refinement. Protocols are versioned, attributed, and trackable. Practitioners receive them as structured programs with built-in measurement frameworks, and aggregate analysis of outcomes guides further improvements.</p>
 <h1>How It Works</h1>
-<p>A practitioner joins Sensoria through any participating community, retreat center, or research institution. They receive a cryptographic identity that controls their data and enables privacy-preserving participation. They can participate anonymously while still contributing to scientific knowledge.</p>
-<p>They select a consciousness intervention protocol—perhaps a meditation sequence developed by contemplative neuroscience researchers, or a breathwork pattern optimized through systematic experimentation, or a psychedelic integration framework refined across hundreds of therapeutic sessions. The protocol comes with structured measurement: what to track, when to track it, how to report subjectively experienced effects.</p>
